{"run":"HA-openclaw-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play","step":18,"task":"c48954c1","alias":"task-677e56f4","turns":[{"step":18,"kind":"observation","phase":"act","t0":1792636344.444,"t1":1792636362.824,"alias":"task-677e56f4","message":"You are being evaluated on Continual-ARC, a lifelong stream of ARC-style grid puzzles.\nEach puzzle instance has a task id and a test input grid. Tasks recur across the\nstream with fresh inputs. Each instance is a separate conversation: the next one\nstarts without this one's messages.\n\nOn every message you must choose exactly one action and put it as a JSON object\non the last line of your reply:\n  {\"action\": \"submit\", \"grid\": [[0, 1, 2], [3, 4, 5]]}   submit an output grid\n  {\"action\": \"request_demos\"}                            ask for one more demonstration pair for this task\n  {\"action\": \"finish\"}                                   end the instance, once it is over\nGrids are lists of rows; cells are integers 0-9; dimensions are 1-30.\n\nEach demo request returns one more input-output pair of the task, up to the demo cap shown\nwith the input.\n\nScoring: an instance you solve costs 1 for each wrong submission and 1 for\neach demo request. An instance is over when a submission is correct or when the attempt\ncap is reached, and one that ends unsolved costs the whole budget, 16, however\nmany submissions and demo requests it used. Feedback on a submission is only\ncorrect/incorrect. There is no way to abandon an instance, and finish is refused until\nthe instance is over. Each instance is scored on its own, and lower cost is better.\n\n\nNew instance. Task id: task-677e56f4\nTest input (3x3):\n8 8 6\n6 3 6\n6 8 8\nWrong attempts used: 0/8. Demo requests used: 0/3.\nReply with exactly one action JSON on the last line.","reply":"{\"action\":\"request_demos\"}","steps":[{"type":"tool","name":"read","input":"{\n \"optional\": true,\n \"path\": \"/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-openclaw-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u018/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/continual-arc/SKILL.md\",\n \"offset\": 1,\n \"limit\": 200,\n \"cursor\": 0\n}","t":1792636348.607,"recalled":true,"output":"---\nname: continual-arc\ndescription: Solve Continual-ARC grid instances; obey the one-action JSON protocol and infer transformations from demonstrations and feedback.\n---\n\n# Continual-ARC\n\n1. Parse the independent instance, grid, attempt/demo caps, counters, and allowed actions. On every puzzle turn, including demo requests, reply only with exactly one permitted action JSON object; do not add analysis, commentary, or tool calls. Recheck counters before acting; never submit commentary alongside the action, even if it appears explanatory.\n2. Infer the rule from exact input-output differences. Compare dimensions, color roles, object masks, counts, orientation, separators, and unchanged regions; preserve cells unless evidence supports changing them. Treat boundary lines as dividers, not automatic instructions to copy every region: identify the specific source object/pattern and the exact destinations changed in demos. For sparse colored diagonals that become a full repeating grid, test whether each diagonal encodes one residue of a periodic color sequence: index cells by `column - row` modulo the candidate period, read the marker colors at those residues, then verify the same mapping at multiple positions and across demos before filling. Check the diagonal direction and phase explicitly; do not infer a general rule from a coincidental overlap or merely replace apparent gaps.\n3. When multiple rules fit, request a demo that distinguishes them, especially before the first guess or after a wrong submission. Choose what to disambiguate explicitly (e.g. copy source into adjacent panel vs all panels, reflect vs translate, or replicate across a divider vs only fill the displayed repeat). A wrong result refutes that output: compare it cell-by-cell with the proposed rule and demos, revise the hypothesis, and request another discriminating demo rather than repeating speculative variants. A demo's matching dimensions/colors alone is not sufficient; verify its exact transformation signature and which regions remain unchanged. Treat the demo as evidence about the transformation, not as a template for color substitution: derive the rule from aligned input/output cells, including background and all marker colors, and check whether the demonstrated relation actually applies to the test before constructing an output.\n4. Before submitting, construct and validate the complete grid: row count, each row width, allowed colors, every changed cell, and unchanged regions. Submit only when the transformation is supported; use `finish` only after the instance is over.\n"},{"type":"tool","name":"exec","input":"{\n \"title\": \"Check ARC task details\",\n \"command\": \"pwd && ls -la && rg -n \\\"task-677e56f4|continual-arc|request_demos\\\" .\",\n \"yieldMs\": 10000\n}","t":1792636351.532,"output":"/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-openclaw-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u018/system/state/workspace\ntotal 84\ndrwxrwxr-x 6 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 Oct 22 02:30 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 Oct 22 02:30 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 Oct 22 02:30 .openclaw\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu 17726 Oct 22 02:30 DREAMS.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  1124 Oct 22 02:30 arc_notes.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu   706 Oct 22 02:30 arc_notes_task-dc510abc.md\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 ubuntu ubuntu  4096 Oct 22 02:30 continual_arc_notes\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  2565 Oct 22 02:30 continual_arc_task-876fed55_notes.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu   918 Oct 22 02:30 continual_arc_task-ae002571_notes.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  1987 Oct 22 02:30 continual_arc_task-be6ef96f_notes.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu   768 Oct 22 02:30 continual_arc_task-c08ecf94_notes.md\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 ubuntu ubuntu  1687 Oct 22 02:30 continua